 ],
 "literal": "Even if resting in the interval between flowers, like a year having passed / like the union of the water-dwelling makanril birds / with unceasing love that is difficult to separate / may our lives depart together! Oh that, knowing our duty / in this world where we were two / we may escape the misery of becoming one!",
 "translation": "Even if they are separated only by the space of a flower, it feels as if a whole year has passed. Like the union of the water-dwelling makanril birds who cannot bear to be apart, may our lives depart together at the same moment with this unceasing, inseparable love! Oh, that we might escape the misery of becoming just one of us left behind in this world where we once lived as two, knowing our duty to each other.",
 "modern": "பூக்களுக்கு இடையே உள்ள சிறிய இடைவெளியில் பிரிந்தாலும் ஒரு வருடம் கழிந்தது போன்ற உணர்வை உண்டாக்கும், நீரில் வாழும் அன்றில் பறவைகளின் இணைபிரியாத சேர்க்கையைப் போல, பிரிவதற்கு அரியதும் குறையாததுமான காதலோடு நம் இருவருடைய உயிரும் ஒரே நேரத்தில் உடலை விட்டுப் போகட்டும்! கடமையை உணர்ந்து, நாம் இருவரும் ஒன்றாக வாழ்ந்த இவ்வுலகில், ஒருவரைப் பிரிந்து மற்றொருவர் மட்டும் தனியாக வாழும் துன்பத்திலிருந்து நாம் தப்பிப்பதற்காக இதுவே வழி.",
 "explanation": "The heroine (talaivi) is expressing her deep love and anxiety about separation to her companion. She compares her love to the legendary makanril birds, which are famous in classical Tamil poetry for their absolute inseparability; even if a flower comes between them, they feel a year-long agony of separation. She wishes that she and her lover could die together at the exact same moment so that neither has to suffer the wretchedness of surviving alone in a world where they once lived happily as a pair. This poem belongs to the neytal tiṇai (seashore landscape), which is characterized by the theme of anxious waiting, pining, and the dread of separation. Key words include makanril (the inseparable bird), taṇṭā kāmam (unceasing love), and uṭaṉuyir pōkuka (may our lives depart together).",
